## Why This Is Asked
This checks practical ability to design reliable, beginner-friendly tests for a time-based data pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Dependency injection for test doubles
- Time control to remove nondeterminism
- In-memory mocks vs real services
- Flake detection and CI gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest example with in-memory queue and fixed time
const { processNext } = require('../src/worker');
const InMemoryQueue = require('../src/mocks').InMemoryQueue;
const redisApi = require('../src/redis');

jest.mock('../src/redis');

test('processNext deterministic time', () => {
  const queue = new InMemoryQueue(['action1','action2']);
  redisApi.getQueue.mockReturnValue(queue);
  jest.useFakeTimers('modern');
  jest.setSystemTime(new Date('2026-01-01T12:00:00Z'));
  processNext();
  expect(queue.processed).toBe(true);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multiple worker instances with separate queues?
- What metrics would indicate flaky tests and how would you triage them?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for feature-flagged checkout in a distributed payment system reflect real-world concerns: rollout safety, concurrency, compliance, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and canary/shadow testing
- Idempotency and race conditions
- End-to-end across services
- Observability and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: idempotent checkout invocation
async function runIdempotentCheckout(key) {
  const first = await checkout.perform({ amount: 1000, customerId: 'C1' }, key);
  const second = await checkout.perform({ amount: 1000, customerId: 'C1' }, key);
  if (first.orderId !== second.orderId) throw new Error('Non-idempotent');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure rollout safety during a transition?
- How would you test in a PCI-compliant environment and ensure no sensitive data is logged?

